"# Snapshot Isolation Analysis — Summary Report\n\n**Workload:** T1 (RW: A, B), T2 (RW: A, B), T3 (RO: A, B) — T1/T2 concurrent off the same snapshot; MVCC with version store A0→A1→A2, B0→B1→B2.\n\n## Isolation Level\n\n**Snapshot Isolation** (snapshot reads + first-committer-wins). Anomaly profile — no dirty reads, no non-repeatable reads, no read skew — rules out anything below SI; the FCW abort of a concurrent writer is SI's signature. It is *not* a Serializable level: the hypothetical both-commit graph contains cycle `T1 ⇄ T2`, proving the guarantee is weaker — serializability here is earned by abort, not guaranteed by the protocol.\n\n## Anomalies\n\n| Anomaly | Verdict | Explanation |\n|---|---|---|\n| Dirty read | Absent | T2.R(A) reads committed A0, never T1's uncommitted A1 |\n| Non-repeatable read | Absent | Single snapshot per transaction |\n| Phantom read | N/A | No predicate scans or inserts/deletes |\n| Write skew | Absent | Concurrent write sets intersect ({A,B} ∩ {A,B}) — degrades to an ordinary ww conflict |\n| Lost update | **Attempted → prevented** | Both increment A, B from the same base; FCW aborts the second committer (T2), so no increment is lost |\n| Read skew | Absent | T3's R(A), R(B) come from one consistent snapshot |\n\n**The single live event: the T1–T2 write-write conflict on both A and B**, resolved as a lost-update attempt that FCW converts into one abort (T2 aborts and retries).\n\n## Serialization Graph & Result\n\n- **Both-commit graph:** edges `T1 →ww(A,B)→ T2` (version order) and `T2 →rw(A,B)→ T1` form the 2-cycle **T1 ⇄ T2** → non-serializable (lost updates on A and B).\n- **Committed projection (FCW aborts T2):** remaining edges {T0→T1, T0→T3, T3→rw→T1} — **acyclic**.\n\n✅ **Serializable.** Equivalent serial schedule: **T3 → T1** (T3 reads the pre-commit snapshot, T1's writes land last). Under the literal as-written reading the history is already the serial schedule **T1 → T2 → T3**.\n\n## Bottom Line\n\nThe committed outcome is serializable at Snapshot Isolation: one writer aborts (T2) and the survivor's schedule collapses to the serial order T3 → T1. The cost is one retry; the caveat is that this clean result is workload-specific — with disjoint write sets the same structure would allow write skew, which only a true Serializable implementation (e.g., SSI) would catch."